+---
+name: code-quality-checklist
+description: Concrete checklist for writing, edge-case-hardening, and testing code changes. Use when implementing a feature, fixing a bug, or refactoring, before declaring the change done.
+---
+
+# Code Quality Checklist
+
+The rule of thumb is "write good code, be careful with edge cases, test things."
+That is too vague to act on, so this skill breaks each part into specific checks.
+Work through all three sections for every non-trivial change.
+
+## 1. Write good code
+
+Before writing:
+- Read the code around the change and copy its conventions (naming, error handling, file layout, formatting). Being consistent with the codebase matters more than personal preference.
+- Search for an existing helper before writing a new one.
+
+While writing:
+- Keep the change scoped to the task. Don't refactor unrelated code or rename things you didn't need to touch.
+- Give functions and variables names that describe what they do or hold. If a name needs a comment to explain it, pick a better name.
+- Handle errors where you can do something useful with them. Don't swallow exceptions silently, and don't catch broad exception types just to make an error go away.
+- Comment on *why*, not *what*: non-obvious constraints, workarounds, and invariants.
+- Don't leave debug prints, commented-out code, or TODOs you could resolve now.
+
+## 2. Be careful with edge cases
+
+For each input, output, and state change, check how the code behaves with:
+
+| Category | Examples |
+|---|---|
+| Empty / missing | empty string, empty list, `null`/`None`/`undefined`, missing key, missing file |
+| Boundaries | 0, 1, -1, max/min int, last index, off-by-one in loops and slices |
+| Size | very large inputs, very long strings, deep nesting |
+| Format | Unicode and emoji, whitespace, mixed case, unexpected types, malformed data |
+| Time | time zones, DST, leap years, ordering, timeouts |
+| Concurrency | repeated or duplicate calls, races, partial failure partway through |
+| External failure | network errors, permission denied, disk full, non-2xx responses |
+
+For each case that applies, decide whether the code should handle it, reject it with a clear error, or document it as out of scope. Make that choice on purpose.
+
+## 3. Test things
+
+- **Run the existing tests** before and after your change. If tests were already failing before you started, note that so you don't mistake them for regressions.
+- **Add tests for the new behavior.** Cover the main path and the edge cases you found in section 2.
+- **For bug fixes, write a test that fails without the fix** and passes with it.
+- **Exercise the real thing when possible.** Run the CLI, call the endpoint, or load the page. Passing unit tests don't prove the feature works.
+- **Run the project's linter, formatter, and type checker** if it has them.
+- **Report results honestly.** If a test fails, show the output. If you skipped something, say what and why. Never claim code is tested when it wasn't.
+
+## Done means
+
+- [ ] The change does what was asked, and nothing it wasn't asked to do
+- [ ] Each relevant edge case is handled, rejected, or documented
+- [ ] New or updated tests cover the change, and the full suite passes
+- [ ] Lint, format, and type checks pass
+- [ ] Any skipped checks or known limitations are stated in your summary
